so as I understand you started this  project because you had a personal  interest in the subject is that the case  yeah I grew up in Philadelphia and I  think due to the nature of the incident  and how traumatic and tragic it was for  the whole city  but also due the fact that I was 11 so I  was roughly the age of some of the  children that died in the house and I  was also at that age where you're really  sort of susceptible to those events that  happen outside it was one of the first  events that I remember that sort of came  from the news that really impacted my my  feeling of well-being if you will my  feeling of safety it really frightened  me as another child living in that city  and I think it just impacted me I didn't  have the sort of ways that adults have  to frame these things and understand  them better to me it was just it was  just frightening like this happened to  me scary and then when I moved away not  that long after you started to talk to  people your age and peers and you  realized this thing that affected you  was largely unknown to other people my  my age and I've talked to over the years  a lot of people across the country and  it's just not that well known among  Americans so I think that sort of added  to it for me and then ultimately I went  to film school and studied documentary  and you you know in film school you  learn a really specific way to sort of  approach these things and process and a  rubric for what makes a good film so it  was sort of this trajectory where it was  like it bothered me as a kid it bothered  me more as a young adult that no one  knew about it and then as a slightly  older young adult I sort of finally had  the tools and the the methods to sort of  approach it through through going to  film school and then beyond that it  still took a long time and I needed an  institutional partner in GW at George  Washington University and and then  ultimately working with knowles and  making the decision to cut this type of  archival film  I personally am fascinated by the way  you have chosen to use only archival  footage in a story that could be told in  a variety of ways how did you make that  decision was that something that else  was involved with I think nose was so  important to that decision that I like  to let him go first on this one i when  when Jason brought the film to me  usually as an editor he come on close to  the end of production but I  Jason had all this footage that he'd  been collecting for years and years and  several interviews contemporary  interviews with the subjects and birdie  Michael Moses ward was one of them James  Burke hired the police officer at the  end was one of them the who are the  other ones Ramona oh yeah Ramona and I  watch these interviews and what struck  me about them is something that maybe  strikes me about this whole subject is  that I don't think anybody understands  what happened now any better than they  did at the time of the incident  and so these interviews were they were  people talking about this now but almost  without much benefit of hindsight people  didn't have a lot more to say about this  so then when I watched the Commission  footage I saw boy this is so it was  surprisingly cinematic with great  lighting and great close-ups and you  know people under pressure to answer  questions why did they have close-ups of  the girl you did you put those in close  the gap no they did a great job  producing this they had cameras lined up  for everything and you know they had a  whole multi camera suite I'm sure  directors you went on for 20 days or so  the Commission so we had lots of lots of  footage of all sorts I got the sense  that the actual production people that  worked on it kind of had a sense of the  moment that that these are guys that do  a lot of sort of you know newsy  public-access c type stuff and here was  this time where it was  I think they sensed it in the same way  and realized it was time to step up to  the plate and and bring their bring  their a-game and saw it as sort of a  creative opportunity or almost a  historic creative opportunity that's the  sense and then we picked that up you  know 25 years later and said we can take  this what they've made and use this  footage the the commissioned footage to  you know give the context and to keep us  in that time period and I suggested that  to Jason and and luckily we agreed that  we thought it was something that we  would give a shot we we thought it was  kind of risky and we said let's let's  try it I mean I think in in some ways it  it puts you into that moment in 1985 but  then in other ways it allows you to draw  your own conclusions from what's going  on I think  we had contemporary interviews in this  it would be certainly about the story  that occurred there but this way you can  see it as what happened on Osage Avenue  in 1985 or you can see it as a parable  for a lot of other things a lot of it  hinged around an archive that was a  Temple University in Philadelphia and  what had happened was the Commission not  everything but not everything you see  but a large portion the Commission as  they were doing their work they went to  the news stations and they asked for the  live tapes as evidence and then all of  that became part of this sort of cache  of stuff that included the well it's a  little more complicated than that but it  included the transcripts and the actual  hearing tapes and these news tapes that  were collected and they were all seated  to a university where they were held in  the library but they were sort of held  under lock and key for a lot of years  and you could sort of you sort of look  but not touch and they were sort of  behind glass and it took a large part of  the battle and I was getting things from  other sources and people were giving me  tapes and and sort of you know minor  stuff but a lot of the time making the  film had to do with negotiating the big  cache of footage out from the archive  that was rather protective of it and  wanted to make sure that they didn't  release it in a way that would cause  legal trouble or I think in a broader  sense caused the story to be told in a  not careful way that's part of archives  nature is famous so the people well and  I should say too what would actually  broke that logjam was going to another  university and joining the faculty of  the George Washington University that  was very very supportive in terms of of  getting the access and negotiating those  deals and it's sort of an interesting  story to me in terms of institutional  support but also you know when I was on  the outside trying to get this access I  really resented that and you know why do  I need this sort of stamp of approval to  make this film but then once you sort of  get on the inside and you do the work to  sort of build a build a career build a  reputation you sort of you know I'm  actually a different person than I was  when I was first trying to access and  you think well maybe there's something  to jumping through those hoops and and  becoming sort of you know making your  bones if you will and getting that  support that you it forces you to  develop in a certain way and gain the  support and sort of  the story in a responsible way
 

